WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.930
The data centre lease ends in March.

2
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:07.040
400 terabytes of history have to move to Google Cloud.

3
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.315
Plus MySQL, SQL Server and a Hadoop cluster.

4
00:00:10.335 --> 00:00:12.625
And not one row can go missing.

5
00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:16.800
Two ideas: how to move the bytes, and how to plan the move.

6
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.210
Before you pick a tool, do the maths.

7
00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.870
One terabyte over a 100 megabit line takes about 1 day.

8
00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.582
So 400 terabytes would take more than a year.

9
00:00:26.602 --> 00:00:28.155
That is no good.

10
00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:32.805
The rule: if the network needs more than about a week, ship a device.

11
00:00:32.825 --> 00:00:34.915
Now match the job to the tool.

12
00:00:34.935 --> 00:00:36.490
Huge data and a slow line?

13
00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:37.765
Transfer Appliance.

14
00:00:37.785 --> 00:00:39.650
Google ships you a box.

15
00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:43.237
Big or repeating file copies, from S3 or on-prem?

16
00:00:43.257 --> 00:00:45.235
Storage Transfer Service.

17
00:00:45.255 --> 00:00:48.572
Moving MySQL to Cloud SQL with almost no downtime?

18
00:00:48.592 --> 00:00:50.845
Database Migration Service.

19
00:00:50.865 --> 00:00:53.508
Streaming every database change into BigQuery?

20
00:00:53.528 --> 00:00:54.980
Datastream.

21
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.805
And BigQuery Data Transfer Service loads tables on a schedule,

22
00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:01.240
from apps and other warehouses.

23
00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.015
The trap: Storage Transfer Service moves files into buckets.

24
00:01:05.035 --> 00:01:08.065
It does not load BigQuery tables.

25
00:01:08.085 --> 00:01:09.727
Idea two: the plan.

26
00:01:09.747 --> 00:01:12.150
Five steps, always in this order.

27
00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.400
Assess what you have.

28
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:14.338
Plan the target.

29
00:01:14.358 --> 00:01:15.588
Migrate in waves.

30
00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.175
Validate against the source.

31
00:01:17.195 --> 00:01:18.760
Then optimize.

32
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.810
Run old and new side by side.

33
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:24.010
That is how you prove no row went missing.

34
00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.347
And the Hadoop cluster?

35
00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:27.770
Swap each part for a managed one.

36
00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.658
Spark jobs go to Dataproc.

37
00:01:29.678 --> 00:01:31.983
HDFS becomes Cloud Storage.

38
00:01:32.003 --> 00:01:34.155
HBase becomes Bigtable.

39
00:01:34.175 --> 00:01:38.540
And your scheduler becomes Cloud Composer, which is managed Airflow.

40
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.978
Keep the data in Cloud Storage, not on cluster disks.

41
00:01:41.998 --> 00:01:45.515
Then you can delete the cluster after each job.

42
00:01:45.535 --> 00:01:46.775
Recap.

43
00:01:46.795 --> 00:01:48.087
Do the maths first.

44
00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:49.825
Match the job to the tool.

45
00:01:49.845 --> 00:01:53.410
And follow the five steps, with validation before the switch.

46
00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.773
That is line one done.

47
00:01:54.793 --> 00:01:57.555
Try the checkpoint below.
